WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.300
You know, you're scrolling through your news feed, looking at the state of the world, watching, I don't know, the latest institutional failure or some global crisis just unfold.

00:08.920 --> 00:14.920
And you get this nagging, almost universal feeling in the pit of your stomach.

00:15.080 --> 00:16.380
Right, that things are falling apart.

00:16.520 --> 00:24.600
Exactly, this feeling that society is in decline, that things used to be better or, you know, simpler, more whole.

00:24.900 --> 00:25.200
Yeah.

00:25.200 --> 00:31.540
And now we're just living in the wreckage of a civilization that has somehow completely lost its way.

00:31.620 --> 00:34.920
I mean, it's something everyone listening to this has probably felt at some point.

00:35.020 --> 00:35.500
Oh, absolutely.

00:35.620 --> 00:39.340
It's a remarkably heavy but very human intuition.

00:39.680 --> 00:44.560
I mean, we are just wired to look backward with nostalgia, right, and forward with anxiety.

00:44.580 --> 00:45.800
Yeah, that's so true.

00:46.040 --> 00:51.400
And we inherently operate on this assumption that a decline implies that we fell from a state of perfection.

00:51.620 --> 00:51.860
Yeah.

00:51.980 --> 00:53.900
Like a lost golden age.

00:53.900 --> 00:54.880
Or like the Garden of Eden.

00:54.880 --> 00:56.420
Exactly, the state of grace.

00:57.200 --> 01:03.980
So we end up measuring our current sickness, our current problems, by comparing it all to this imagined historical health.

01:04.380 --> 01:10.100
But what if that entirely intuitive, deeply human feeling is completely backward?

01:10.640 --> 01:15.160
What if a falling empire is actually healthier than a peaceful, stagnant one?

01:15.160 --> 01:20.300
Because today we are dismantling how you think about civilizational health and sickness.

01:20.620 --> 01:29.700
We've got an intensely fascinating September 2026 paper by independent researcher Flyxion, and it's titled Decadence as Transformation.

01:29.700 --> 01:33.060
Nietzsche, Voltaire, and Rousseau on Decline.

01:33.160 --> 01:34.900
It's a brilliant piece of work.

01:35.020 --> 01:35.740
It really is.

01:35.880 --> 01:39.720
And the mission for this deep dive is to completely strip away that nostalgia.

01:39.800 --> 01:50.260
We are going to look at how the philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche saw decadence not as a fall from innocence, but as the failure of a system to, well, to regenerate its own energy.

01:50.260 --> 01:56.640
Yeah, and to really understand this new definition of decadence, we first have to, like, examine the geometry of history.

01:56.800 --> 01:57.300
The geometry.

01:57.400 --> 01:58.760
Yeah, the underlying shape of it.

01:59.000 --> 02:03.380
We have to map out the assumptions we make about how human progress or regression actually moves.

02:03.460 --> 02:03.640
Right.

02:03.960 --> 02:10.860
And we can start by looking at Jean-Jacques Rousseau, because his model of history still unconsciously dominates how most of us think today.

02:11.160 --> 02:12.800
Okay, so what is his model?

02:12.800 --> 02:15.540
It operates on a very specific mathematical structure.

02:15.860 --> 02:17.820
It's called a conserved origin genealogy.

02:18.240 --> 02:21.500
You can visualize it as a straight line, like an arrow.

02:21.700 --> 02:22.520
Okay, an arrow.

02:22.580 --> 02:28.240
At the very start of the arrow, you have what we can call H-Net, which stands for natural humanity.

02:28.340 --> 02:29.300
The starting point.

02:29.400 --> 02:29.600
Right.

02:29.680 --> 02:36.460
And then you have the intervention of society, right, the passage of time, which leads you all the way to the end of the arrow.

02:36.880 --> 02:39.940
And that's H-Core, or corrupted humanity.

02:39.940 --> 02:44.940
So, natural humanity transforms into corrupted humanity simply through the process of socialization.

02:45.880 --> 02:46.200
Exactly.

02:46.580 --> 02:51.840
It's like, I don't know, it's like buying a pristine, brand-new smartphone straight out of the box.

02:51.940 --> 02:53.000
Oh, that's a great analogy.

02:53.160 --> 02:55.740
You know that moment you peel the plastic film off the screen?

02:55.980 --> 02:56.920
So satisfying.

02:57.120 --> 02:57.380
Right.

02:57.540 --> 03:00.420
But that exact second, that is the origin point.

03:00.540 --> 03:01.600
The perfect state.

03:01.700 --> 03:04.640
From that second onward, every scratch on the screen.

03:04.660 --> 03:06.500
Every software update that slows it down.

03:06.500 --> 03:06.900
Yes.

03:07.100 --> 03:11.540
Every time the battery capacity drops, that is the decline from its original perfection.

03:11.760 --> 03:16.860
Rousseau is using this original, unblemished baseline as his ultimate moral standard.

03:17.100 --> 03:17.580
He is.

03:18.120 --> 03:21.820
And that baseline becomes the sole metric for critique.

03:22.660 --> 03:33.720
I mean, if you accept a conserved origin genealogy, your only method for evaluating society is to measure the distance between where you are now and that original, innocent state.

03:33.800 --> 03:35.400
Because the origin is pure.

03:35.400 --> 03:36.000
Right.

03:36.440 --> 03:37.320
Inherently pure.

03:37.600 --> 03:40.840
And whatever deviates from it is degraded by definition.

03:41.040 --> 03:43.200
And you see this everywhere today, don't you?

03:43.340 --> 03:44.020
Oh, constantly.

03:44.300 --> 03:54.300
The assumption that natural foods, natural habitats, or, you know, ancient tribal social structures were fundamentally more wholesome and less alienating than whatever we've built today.

03:54.380 --> 03:57.200
It basically assumes civilization itself is a contaminant.

03:57.340 --> 03:57.700
Exactly.

03:58.020 --> 04:01.600
But then Nietzsche comes along and introduces a totally different geometric shape.

04:01.680 --> 04:01.920
Right.

04:02.000 --> 04:03.300
He throws out the straight line.

04:03.300 --> 04:05.480
He totally shatters the pristine smartphone.

04:06.020 --> 04:11.600
He operates on what the text calls a transformational genealogy, which completely removes the perfect baseline.

04:11.880 --> 04:12.460
Just wipes it out.

04:12.560 --> 04:13.000
Wipes it out.

04:13.380 --> 04:14.460
There is no H-net.

04:14.960 --> 04:21.660
There is no innocent, natural humanity waiting to be uncovered if we just, you know, stripped away all the skyscrapers and the Internet.

04:21.660 --> 04:27.500
So if it's not a straight line from purity to corruption, what does Nietzsche's model look like?

04:27.800 --> 04:29.940
It's a continuous, self-propelling chain.

04:30.400 --> 04:30.480
Yeah.

04:30.520 --> 04:33.220
You start with a population of drives at a given time in history.

04:33.600 --> 04:35.600
Let's designate this as D sub T.

04:35.660 --> 04:36.260
D sub T.

04:36.440 --> 04:36.580
Okay.

04:36.580 --> 04:39.400
And these drives are just raw, chaotic forces.

04:39.980 --> 04:46.100
Things like cruelty, the instinct for imitation, fear, the desire for appropriation.

04:46.160 --> 04:47.340
Like creative aggression.

04:47.620 --> 04:47.940
Yes.

04:48.120 --> 04:48.440
Yes.

04:48.440 --> 04:51.300
And there is no inherent morality to them, right?

04:51.300 --> 04:53.500
They are just vectors of human energy.

04:53.800 --> 04:55.240
But they don't just sit there in a vacuum.

04:55.520 --> 04:58.060
They run into the structures of civilization.

04:58.440 --> 04:58.780
Precisely.

04:59.000 --> 05:02.340
They undergo a transformation mechanism, which we can call T sub T.

05:02.340 --> 05:02.820
Okay.

05:03.040 --> 05:05.300
So D sub T hits T sub T.

05:05.480 --> 05:05.700
Right.

05:06.280 --> 05:08.280
Society acts upon these raw drives.

05:08.440 --> 05:13.620
It inhibits them, ranks them, redirects them, or sublimates them.

05:13.640 --> 05:14.800
Can you give an example of that?

05:14.880 --> 05:15.740
Like sublimation.

05:15.840 --> 05:16.060
Sure.

05:16.180 --> 05:19.160
So imagine the raw, violent aggression of an ancient warrior culture.

05:19.280 --> 05:19.520
Okay.

05:19.840 --> 05:24.820
Now, that drive encounters the transformation mechanism of organized religion and state law.

05:25.000 --> 05:26.540
The drive doesn't just disappear.

05:26.620 --> 05:27.440
It gets channeled.

05:27.600 --> 05:28.000
Exactly.

05:28.240 --> 05:29.660
It gets sublimated into something else.

05:29.660 --> 05:36.140
Maybe it becomes the disciplined asceticism of a monk, or even the hyper-competitive drive

05:36.140 --> 05:37.580
of a modern corporate executive.

05:37.900 --> 05:38.200
Wow.

05:38.480 --> 05:38.700
Okay.

05:39.060 --> 05:43.500
And that transformation produces a totally new configuration of drives, which we'd call

05:43.500 --> 05:44.980
D sub T plus one.

05:45.120 --> 05:45.960
The next state.

05:46.240 --> 05:46.440
Right.

05:46.520 --> 05:48.480
And then the whole process just happens again.

05:48.760 --> 05:48.960
Yeah.

05:49.140 --> 05:54.120
The new configuration is acted upon by new societal mechanisms to produce the next state.

05:54.120 --> 05:58.820
So history is really just a chain of configurations, each one produced from the last.

05:58.820 --> 06:04.740
But wait, if there's no original state of innocence, like you said, how can Nietzsche

06:04.740 --> 06:08.340
even talk about decline or exhaustion?

06:08.720 --> 06:09.220
What do you mean?

06:09.540 --> 06:13.900
Well, if there's no pristine smartphone out of the box, what makes the current phone broken?

06:14.140 --> 06:17.860
Like, you can't say society is sick if you don't even have a blueprint for what a healthy

06:17.860 --> 06:18.920
society looks like.

06:18.960 --> 06:20.040
That's the crucial shift.

06:20.540 --> 06:24.300
For Nietzsche, evaluating a culture isn't about comparing it to a lost Eden.

06:24.880 --> 06:26.740
You aren't looking backward at all.

06:26.800 --> 06:27.800
So what are you looking at?

06:27.800 --> 06:33.720
You're looking at a specific current configuration of forces, and you're asking a strictly internal

06:33.720 --> 06:34.220
question.

06:35.280 --> 06:38.860
What forms of life does this current configuration permit?

06:39.180 --> 06:39.620
Oh.

06:39.620 --> 06:46.580
If there is no perfect past to compare ourselves to, decadence cannot be a measurement of distance

06:46.580 --> 06:47.180
from nature.

06:47.820 --> 06:50.180
It has to be an internal evaluation.

06:50.320 --> 06:52.460
You are evaluating the system from the inside.

06:52.980 --> 06:54.340
We aren't mourning the past.

06:54.460 --> 06:56.640
We are, like, auditing the present.

06:56.760 --> 06:57.640
Auditing the present.

06:57.780 --> 06:58.260
I like that.

06:58.400 --> 07:02.020
And this leads right to the formal definition of decadence in the text, right?

07:02.140 --> 07:06.780
It says decadence is a relationship between a configuration and its own conditions of production.

07:06.780 --> 07:08.320
It's all about self-negation.

07:08.440 --> 07:09.760
So how does that look in practice?

07:10.300 --> 07:15.360
Well, think about what it takes to build a complex civilization or an empire or, you know,

07:15.360 --> 07:17.200
even a highly successful company.

07:17.280 --> 07:18.140
It takes a lot of work.

07:18.140 --> 07:21.040
Enormous constraint, tension, delayed gratification.

07:21.940 --> 07:26.020
And it takes the sublimation of those raw, aggressive drives we mentioned earlier.

07:26.320 --> 07:33.620
So a culture is generative, meaning it is healthy if it sustains the very drives and tensions that

07:33.620 --> 07:40.900
produced it, if it remains capable of yielding, demanding, difficult, exceptional forms of life.

07:40.900 --> 07:45.260
But it becomes depotent when its values turn against those originating conditions.

07:45.600 --> 07:51.260
Like, a society builds its wealth and safety through immense struggle and risk-taking.

07:51.500 --> 07:53.020
But once it achieves that safety...

07:53.020 --> 07:53.760
Once they get comfortable.

07:54.060 --> 08:00.920
Yeah, they get comfortable and it starts rewarding absolute conformity and extreme risk aversion and universalized pity,

08:01.360 --> 08:07.480
it actively suppresses all the exceptional abrasive forms of life that built the safety in the first place.

08:07.480 --> 08:11.740
It's essentially consuming the fuel that built it without mining any new fuel.

08:11.820 --> 08:12.040
Right.

08:12.200 --> 08:14.580
Which means decadence is not a state you are in.

08:14.880 --> 08:16.400
It is a trajectory you are on.

08:16.540 --> 08:18.300
That is such a key distinction.

08:18.440 --> 08:19.160
A trajectory.

08:19.360 --> 08:23.640
Yeah, you cannot look at a static snapshot of a society to diagnose decadence.

08:23.840 --> 08:26.580
And this is actually formalized in the paper as Proposition 2.

08:26.680 --> 08:27.560
Right, I remember that.

08:27.560 --> 08:36.980
It says you cannot just look at a culture's level of luxury or its leisure time or its artistic output in a single year

08:36.980 --> 08:41.080
or even its religious observances and just declare it decadent.

08:41.160 --> 08:44.100
Because the snapshot doesn't tell you the direction things are moving.

08:44.240 --> 08:44.620
Exactly.

08:44.860 --> 08:47.000
It's like, let's say you're looking at two checking accounts.

08:47.400 --> 08:47.580
Okay.

08:47.800 --> 08:51.000
And they both have exactly $10,000 in them.

08:51.080 --> 08:51.260
Right.

08:51.620 --> 08:54.040
The snapshot is mathematically identical.

08:54.320 --> 09:00.720
But one account is actively depositing $500 a month and the other account is bleeding $500 a month.

09:00.940 --> 09:01.340
Bleeding out.

09:01.340 --> 09:09.700
The static number, the $10,000, or, you know, in a society's case, the massive monuments and the comfortable citizens, that means absolutely nothing.

09:09.840 --> 09:10.540
It means nothing.

09:10.720 --> 09:15.340
The trajectory, the rate of change, that tells the real story of financial health.

09:15.520 --> 09:16.780
That's a perfect way to put it.

09:17.320 --> 09:22.360
You could have two identically comfortable, luxurious societies.

09:22.360 --> 09:29.660
But one is actively regenerating the capacities that built it, while the other is just coasting on historical momentum.

09:29.960 --> 09:31.180
And depleting its capacities.

09:31.680 --> 09:32.640
Depleting them, yes.

09:33.160 --> 09:37.380
The static features, the wealth and the comfort, those are not the decadence.

09:37.740 --> 09:39.200
Decadence is a sign of the derivative.

09:39.420 --> 09:40.980
The mathematical rate of change.

09:41.180 --> 09:41.440
Yes.

09:41.620 --> 09:44.920
And the paper actually provides a formula for this, doesn't it?

09:44.960 --> 09:45.580
Equation one.

09:45.740 --> 09:50.380
It translates this whole abstract philosophical concept into concrete mechanics.

09:50.640 --> 09:51.060
It does.

09:51.060 --> 09:55.780
It says the rate of change in a society's generative capacity, which is delta X sub T.

09:56.040 --> 09:57.680
Okay, delta X sub T.

09:57.780 --> 10:02.120
It's equal to G minus R. Regeneration minus depletion.

10:02.200 --> 10:04.740
So delta X sub T equals G minus R.

10:04.880 --> 10:08.120
Yes, or DC over DT equals G minus R.

10:08.500 --> 10:13.740
If regeneration, the G, outpaces depletion, the R, you have a positive trajectory.

10:13.980 --> 10:18.280
And that's regardless of how much constraint or hardship exists in that society.

10:18.280 --> 10:21.500
Right, but if depletion persistently outpaces regeneration.

10:22.100 --> 10:23.340
Then you have a negative trajectory.

10:24.000 --> 10:26.820
And that negative trajectory is the structural definition of decadence.

10:27.180 --> 10:31.280
It means the system's organization is increasingly acting against the conditions of its own production.

10:31.440 --> 10:36.660
And what I found so vital in this paper is how the text breaks down that depletion variable, the R.

10:36.740 --> 10:37.460
It's very nuanced.

10:37.460 --> 10:43.300
Because depletion isn't just, you know, a barbarian horde coming in and actively destroying a city.

10:43.440 --> 10:43.660
Right.

10:43.740 --> 10:45.100
It's not always violent destruction.

10:45.240 --> 10:45.420
No.

10:45.840 --> 10:48.460
The paper specifies three components of depletion.

10:49.240 --> 10:58.380
Destroying an existing capacity, preventing the exercise of a capacity, or, and this is the big one, preventing the formation of a capacity in the first place.

10:58.380 --> 11:04.080
That third component, preventing the formation of capacity, that is the most insidious form of decadence.

11:04.360 --> 11:05.320
Why is that?

11:05.600 --> 11:16.320
Because a society can be deeply decadent simply by organizing itself in a way that stops its citizens from ever developing resilience, or cognitive endurance, or greatness.

11:16.640 --> 11:18.440
So it doesn't even require active oppression.

11:18.780 --> 11:19.320
Not at all.

11:19.600 --> 11:24.460
It merely requires an environment so devoid of friction that the muscles of capacity just never form.

11:24.520 --> 11:25.020
Wow.

11:25.020 --> 11:28.300
Furthermore, this highlights a really vital nuance about the R variable.

11:29.060 --> 11:33.340
Heavy constraint or intense social pressure isn't necessarily decadent on its own.

11:33.580 --> 11:33.860
Right.

11:33.960 --> 11:37.700
A society can have massive constraints, like a very large R value.

11:38.260 --> 11:48.800
But as long as its regenerative engines, its G, are keeping pace and producing incredible cultural or spiritual output from that tension, it's not decadent.

11:49.140 --> 11:49.640
Exactly.

11:49.800 --> 11:50.840
Think of the Roman Republic.

11:51.000 --> 11:51.240
Okay.

11:51.240 --> 11:56.200
They had immense social constraints, brutal military expectations, strict hierarchies.

11:56.380 --> 12:01.440
But that intense friction regenerated their capacity to expand and build.

12:01.660 --> 12:02.740
But then what happened?

12:03.460 --> 12:09.480
Well, it was only when the friction was removed by total dominance and massive wealth that the regeneration stopped.

12:09.840 --> 12:12.900
The wealth remained high, but the trajectory went negative.

12:13.060 --> 12:14.120
That makes so much sense.

12:14.120 --> 12:21.040
Okay, so we've established how history is measured and how decadence is defined mathematically, basically as a failure to regenerate.

12:21.440 --> 12:28.820
But these historical models, Rousseau's pristine past versus Nietzsche's changed transformations, they are just academic theories locked away in some library.

12:29.020 --> 12:30.000
Oh, no, not at all.

12:30.260 --> 12:32.380
They are deeply potent psychological tools.

12:32.500 --> 12:32.700
Right.

12:32.940 --> 12:37.340
They are used by individuals and groups to gain power and to navigate their own suffering.

12:37.620 --> 12:40.120
This is what the paper calls the alchemy of resentment.

12:40.560 --> 12:41.880
The alchemy of resentment.

12:41.880 --> 12:43.260
I love that phrasing.

12:43.260 --> 12:44.080
It's fascinating.

12:44.800 --> 12:52.080
It hears how a raw personal feeling of injury is magically transmuted into a universal political doctrine.

12:52.960 --> 12:59.340
The paper traces Rousseau's political operation, at least as Nietzsche interpreted it, through a very specific sequence.

12:59.500 --> 13:00.280
What's the sequence?

13:00.640 --> 13:02.320
Subordination leads to resentment.

13:03.000 --> 13:05.140
Resentment generates a moral interpretation.

13:05.960 --> 13:09.600
That moral interpretation crystallizes into a universal principle.

13:09.600 --> 13:13.760
And finally, that universal principle provides political legitimacy.

13:14.000 --> 13:17.600
OK, let's actually trace the mechanics of the psychology because it is just profound.

13:18.320 --> 13:18.800
It really is.

13:18.960 --> 13:22.680
So imagine an individual in a lower social position, right?

13:22.900 --> 13:24.820
Lacking power, wealth, status.

13:25.180 --> 13:30.300
The raw grievance inside them is simply those people above me have advantages and resources that I do not.

13:30.420 --> 13:30.640
Right.

13:30.640 --> 13:34.720
But as a political rallying cry, that raw statement is incredibly weak.

13:34.800 --> 13:35.360
It's ins weak.

13:35.460 --> 13:38.040
And honestly, it's psychologically agonizing.

13:38.460 --> 13:38.940
Exactly.

13:39.180 --> 13:46.640
Because if you walk around simply saying, I am angry because they are better off than me, you are implicitly conceding the validity of the hierarchy.

13:46.800 --> 13:51.960
You're admitting that they have achieved something you haven't and that you are just suffering from envy.

13:51.960 --> 13:56.280
Which is an intolerable state for the human ego to remain in for very long.

13:56.440 --> 13:59.420
The ego demands a mechanism to relieve that tension.

13:59.740 --> 14:03.100
And this is where the third and fourth steps of the sequence come in.

14:03.460 --> 14:09.580
The individual flips that raw grievance into a moral interpretation and then into a universal principle.

14:09.700 --> 14:09.880
Yes.

14:10.260 --> 14:10.840
They declare,

14:10.840 --> 14:25.280
It's an absolute magic trick of the human mind.

14:25.460 --> 14:26.620
It really is a sleight of hand.

14:26.800 --> 14:32.380
Suddenly, your personal uncomfortable resentment is transformed into profound moral insight.

14:33.020 --> 14:35.580
You aren't just a jealous person at the bottom of a ladder anymore.

14:35.580 --> 14:40.120
You are the vanguard of human nature, speaking out against the artificial corruption of the elites.

14:40.480 --> 14:40.580
Right.

14:40.840 --> 14:46.460
The particular selfish interest vanishes completely into a grand claim about humanity itself.

14:46.460 --> 14:51.180
So the lower position in the hierarchy becomes presumptive evidence of innocence.

14:51.780 --> 14:57.800
And the refined elite position becomes presumptive evidence of corruption.

14:58.680 --> 15:05.380
A purely descriptive category, you know, who has power and who does not, has been weaponized into a normative standard of good and evil.

15:05.660 --> 15:06.820
That is so wild.

15:07.040 --> 15:10.160
And this leads right into how Nietzsche diagnoses philosophy.

15:10.160 --> 15:12.620
He treats these doctrines as symptoms.

15:12.940 --> 15:13.020
Yes.

15:13.260 --> 15:16.260
He doesn't just look at a philosophy and ask, is this logically true?

15:16.720 --> 15:22.820
He asks, what is the physiological and psychological condition of the person for whom this doctrine feels true?

15:23.040 --> 15:25.160
He's investigating the utility of the belief.

15:25.160 --> 15:25.720
Exactly.

15:26.100 --> 15:37.600
If millions of people suddenly adopt a philosophy that claims all hierarchy is evil and all inequality is a deviation from nature, Nietzsche looks at what that belief actually accomplishes for the believers.

15:37.720 --> 15:38.800
What does it do for them?

15:39.000 --> 15:40.240
It acts as an anesthetic.

15:40.240 --> 15:47.100
It consoles their resentment and gives them a narrative where they are the righteous victims rather than the failures of a competitive system.

15:47.100 --> 15:55.340
Okay, we need to pause here for a second and just test the boundaries of this idea because I want to make sure we're being completely impartial here and looking at this objectively.

15:55.340 --> 16:04.540
Let's say I am working 80 hours a week in a brutal, completely unregulated factory, barely making enough to feed my family.

16:04.800 --> 16:08.980
And meanwhile, the factory owner is extracting massive wealth for my labor.

16:08.980 --> 16:16.280
However, if I express anger at that system, you can't just dismiss my anger as Rousseauan resentment.

16:16.520 --> 16:17.220
Right, of course not.

16:17.360 --> 16:18.960
I am genuinely suffering.

16:19.100 --> 16:20.560
The exploitation is real.

16:21.060 --> 16:28.040
How does Nietzsche account for actual undeniable oppression without just, you know, writing it off as the whining of the week?

16:28.160 --> 16:30.740
That is the exact friction point of this theory.

16:30.920 --> 16:33.980
And the text actually addresses this limitation very carefully.

16:34.240 --> 16:34.980
Oh, it does.

16:34.980 --> 16:38.720
Yes, because Nietzsche is not denying the reality of suffering.

16:38.980 --> 16:42.240
The physical pain, the starvation, the exhaustion of the factory worker.

16:42.420 --> 16:44.280
That is empirical reality.

16:44.400 --> 16:46.120
Okay, so he admits the suffering is real.

16:46.280 --> 16:46.560
Yes.

16:47.060 --> 16:47.980
And this is the key.

16:48.120 --> 16:49.500
Suffering is not self-interpreting.

16:49.600 --> 16:50.480
Wait, meaning what?

16:50.580 --> 16:55.220
The pain is real, but the story I tell myself about why I'm in pain is constructed?

16:55.760 --> 16:56.280
Precisely.

16:56.280 --> 17:06.920
The experience of suffering under a particular social arrangement does not automatically prove that the arrangement is a degeneration from some natural, healthy state of equality.

17:07.180 --> 17:07.540
Oh.

17:07.540 --> 17:09.640
The suffering is a fact.

17:09.640 --> 17:20.220
But the causal history, the narrative that says we used to be equal in the state of nature and this factory owner has corrupted that natural law, that is an interpretation.

17:20.880 --> 17:32.880
So Nietzsche's point is that your grievance against the factory owner can be entirely justified and you can fight to overthrow that system without having to invent a fabricated theory of human nature to legitimize your fight.

17:32.880 --> 17:37.260
Exactly. You can say, this system is destroying my capacity to live and I'm going to change it.

17:37.640 --> 17:41.460
You don't need to claim, I am restoring the pure innocence of our ancestors.

17:41.660 --> 17:42.520
That makes a lot of sense.

17:42.820 --> 17:48.780
And this brings up the genetic fallacy, which the paper highlights as a crucial philosophical trap to avoid here.

17:48.780 --> 17:56.580
Right. The genetic fallacy occurs when you assume that by exposing the psychological origins of a belief, you have automatically proven the belief false.

17:56.580 --> 18:04.580
So, if Nietzsche successfully proves that a doctrine of equality is merely a psychological coping mechanism for the resentful...

18:05.080 --> 18:10.820
He hasn't actually disproven the mathematical or ethical validity of equality itself.

18:10.820 --> 18:16.780
Explaining why a belief is attractive to a sufferer does not logically refute the doctrine.

18:17.180 --> 18:19.660
It simply explains its contiguous appeal.

18:19.660 --> 18:26.620
It explains why a narrative that might not hold up to rigorous historical scrutiny nonetheless captures the hearts of millions of people.

18:26.900 --> 18:27.980
Right. It makes them feel better.

18:28.300 --> 18:36.320
But if Rousseau's nostalgia is a psychological trap and we can't look back to a golden age for our moral compass, where do we look?

18:36.400 --> 18:37.220
We look to Voltaire.

18:37.340 --> 18:37.780
Voltaire.

18:37.780 --> 18:43.900
Yes. It is highly significant that Nietzsche actually dedicated his book, Human, All Too Human, to Voltaire.

18:44.020 --> 18:45.240
Really? I didn't know that.

18:45.360 --> 18:50.780
Yeah. It signals a deep methodological allegiance because Voltaire represents emancipation by disillusionment.

18:51.720 --> 18:52.240
Disillusionment.

18:52.280 --> 18:54.140
It is a strictly subtractive method.

18:54.240 --> 18:59.420
Right. Because both Rousseau and Voltaire wanted to strip away the suffocating artifice of society.

18:59.720 --> 19:04.180
You know, the religious dogmas, the fake aristocratic manners, the arbitrary laws.

19:04.180 --> 19:08.240
But they expected to find completely different things once they pulled the curtain back.

19:08.320 --> 19:16.760
Exactly. Rousseau expected that if you stripped away the corruption of society, you would uncover this benign, pure, innocent human nature.

19:16.960 --> 19:22.920
Whereas Voltaire and, by extension, Nietzsche, perform the exact same subtractive operation.

19:22.920 --> 19:30.160
They ruthlessly strip away the falsehoods and the comforting myths, but they don't expect to find innocence beneath it.

19:30.640 --> 19:31.840
What do they expect to find?

19:31.920 --> 19:34.200
A field of chaotic competing forces.

19:34.720 --> 19:38.260
Forces that have absolutely no obligation to be benign or fair.

19:38.540 --> 19:42.160
So it is disillusionment, but without the romantic safety net.

19:42.160 --> 19:50.720
Exactly. And this introduces a massive formal obstacle to the entire concept of traditionalism, or the whole idea of going back to a better time.

19:50.900 --> 19:58.060
The paper points out that psychological transformations, like the sublimation of those raw drives we talked about earlier, are many-to-one transformations.

19:58.440 --> 20:08.080
Right. In mathematics and complex systems, a many-to-one transformation means that as the system moves from state A to state B, information about its earlier state is lost.

20:08.220 --> 20:09.220
It's permanently gone.

20:09.220 --> 20:12.960
Yes. The transformation does not have an inverse function.

20:13.480 --> 20:17.620
You cannot simply reverse the equation to arrive back at the exact starting point.

20:17.700 --> 20:18.960
Think of it like baking, right?

20:19.100 --> 20:19.840
Okay, let's hear it.

20:20.240 --> 20:28.160
If you take raw eggs, flour, sugar, and butter, and you subject them to the transformation mechanism of an oven...

20:28.160 --> 20:28.800
You get a cake.

20:28.900 --> 20:31.940
You get a cake. And that is a many-to-one transformation.

20:32.540 --> 20:35.180
The chemical process is totally irreversible.

20:35.180 --> 20:40.900
The information of the separated ingredients is permanently lost in the passage of heat.

20:41.220 --> 20:41.680
That's brilliant.

20:41.780 --> 20:45.780
You cannot unbake the cake to get your pristine original eggs back.

20:45.920 --> 20:48.900
And the history of human psychology works the exact same way.

20:49.600 --> 20:58.040
Once a civilization has internalized a certain moral framework or developed a certain level of self-consciousness, the previous state is gone.

20:58.040 --> 21:01.420
So going backward is not just morally undesirable.

21:01.640 --> 21:05.120
It is structurally undefined. The operation simply cannot be performed.

21:05.240 --> 21:12.080
Which means the remedy for a decadent society, a society that is failing to regenerate its capacities, cannot be restoration.

21:12.480 --> 21:15.580
No. Restoration is a fantasy of the unbaked cake.

21:16.140 --> 21:18.780
What remains is what Nietzsche calls revaluation.

21:18.780 --> 21:19.260
Revaluation.

21:19.780 --> 21:20.180
Revaluation.

21:20.660 --> 21:27.940
Yes. The deliberate production of a new configuration of values and drives that has never existed before.

21:28.100 --> 21:32.280
I love how the paper highlights a moment where Nietzsche almost catches himself in his own writing.

21:32.660 --> 21:34.880
Oh yeah, he starts to speak of a return to nature.

21:35.000 --> 21:36.980
But then he immediately stops and corrects himself.

21:36.980 --> 21:43.440
He does. He explicitly states it is not a going back to nature, but a climbing up.

21:43.440 --> 21:48.860
You have to invent an entirely new dish using the ingredients of the present moment.

21:49.340 --> 21:50.600
You have to build a new scaffolding.

21:50.780 --> 21:51.840
Which is a perfect segue.

21:52.080 --> 21:58.120
Right. This brings us to the most practical, immediately applicable section of the paper, the appendix.

21:58.280 --> 22:06.600
Because we need to talk about how this extremely dense structural mathematics of decadence applies to the actual lives of the people listening to this right now.

22:06.680 --> 22:07.340
Highly applicable.

22:07.620 --> 22:11.640
We talked about the regeneration equation where capacity is equal to G minus R.

22:11.640 --> 22:16.380
Let's apply that to how we interact with our environments, our workplaces, and our families today.

22:16.700 --> 22:24.200
We can connect Nietzsche's concept of capacity regeneration directly to modern concepts of load-bearing and antifragility.

22:24.500 --> 22:25.720
Okay, break that down for us.

22:25.800 --> 22:33.520
Biological systems, psychological systems, cognitive systems, they all gain capacity precisely through exposure to stressors.

22:33.720 --> 22:35.320
The friction is the signal.

22:35.540 --> 22:39.620
Yes. The friction is the signal that tells the system to build more capacity.

22:39.620 --> 22:54.480
So if you build an environment that is perfectly optimized to remove all discomfort, you know, a frictionless, climate-controlled, hyper-convenient, modern lifestyle, you are actively lowering your regeneration rate.

22:54.520 --> 22:56.860
You are dropping your G variable to zero.

22:57.020 --> 22:57.380
Wow.

22:57.380 --> 23:05.200
Because the dominant valuation in modern affluent societies is the maximization of comfort and the minimization of risk.

23:05.800 --> 23:11.300
But comfort, when pursued as an absolute endpoint, actually consumes its own conditions of production.

23:11.440 --> 23:14.660
By systematically removing the stressors on which regeneration depends.

23:14.760 --> 23:18.200
Exactly. The society is acting against its own long-term survival.

23:18.400 --> 23:22.480
The text points out that this gives actual structural weight to the term coddling.

23:22.680 --> 23:23.460
It really does.

23:23.460 --> 23:27.300
Because when people talk about modern coddling, they're usually just being dismissive, right?

23:27.440 --> 23:29.500
Like calling younger generations soft.

23:29.640 --> 23:30.860
Yeah, it's usually just an insult.

23:31.140 --> 23:35.000
But through this framework, it's not a moral judgment about character.

23:35.360 --> 23:40.000
It's a literal mathematical claim about the trajectory of a person's capacity.

23:40.680 --> 23:44.440
The arrangement of their life is preventing the formation of capacity.

23:44.440 --> 23:51.040
And to illustrate this practically, the text draws a formal distinction between two types of developmental environments.

23:51.280 --> 23:52.720
The scaffold and the cocoon.

23:53.100 --> 23:59.800
Right. And this applies to raising children, managing employees, or even structuring an educational curriculum.

23:59.980 --> 24:04.780
Let's look at the scaffolds first. How does the math work in a healthy developmental environment?

24:05.180 --> 24:10.560
Okay. So you have a degree of protection being provided by the parent or manager, which we'll call P.

24:10.760 --> 24:11.620
P for protection.

24:11.620 --> 24:15.320
And you have the current level of capacity in the learner, which is C.

24:15.880 --> 24:24.320
In a scaffold arrangement, there is an optimal dynamic level of protection, let's call it P star, that correctly tracks with the rising capacity.

24:24.580 --> 24:32.720
Meaning as the capacity, the C of the learner increases, the level of protection, the P, must proportionally decrease.

24:32.860 --> 24:33.340
Exactly.

24:33.500 --> 24:36.000
It is the classic training wheels analogy, isn't it?

24:36.000 --> 24:41.240
It is. When a child first gets on a bike, their capacity for balance is zero.

24:41.660 --> 24:44.680
So you provide maximum protection, the training wheels.

24:44.920 --> 24:54.020
Right. But as their neurological capacity to balance rises, if you leave the training wheels firmly on the ground, you are actively impeding their progress.

24:54.160 --> 24:59.340
You are. You have to raise the wheels slightly, introducing a stressor, a risk of falling.

24:59.340 --> 25:05.680
And as capacity rises, protection drops until the wheels come off entirely. That is a generative arrangement.

25:06.060 --> 25:11.040
But then we encounter the cocoon, which is mathematically and psychologically disastrous.

25:11.380 --> 25:12.420
What happens at a cocoon?

25:12.700 --> 25:20.540
A cocoon occurs when the level of protection provided remains persistently higher than the optimal level, regardless of the learner's underlying capacity.

25:20.540 --> 25:27.540
And the real danger of the cocoon isn't just that the person is overly protected, right? It's the self-undermining feedback loop it generates.

25:27.720 --> 25:29.260
Oh, the feedback loop is brutal.

25:29.500 --> 25:33.780
Let's trace this loop, because it is incredibly common in modern management and parenting.

25:34.700 --> 25:38.460
Step one. Protection goes up artificially high.

25:38.780 --> 25:45.480
And because protection is artificially high, the individual's exposure to natural stressors, friction, and failure.

25:45.580 --> 25:46.420
It drops to zero.

25:46.420 --> 25:51.200
Right. And because exposure drops, their internal capacity development stalls.

25:51.860 --> 25:57.120
They aren't learning how to handle difficulty or navigate conflict or recover from mistakes.

25:57.360 --> 25:59.660
So their capacity remains artificially low.

25:59.840 --> 26:06.500
And because their capacity is now visibly stunted, it creates the overwhelming apparent need for even more protection.

26:06.820 --> 26:10.480
The system creates the exact weakness that justifies the system.

26:10.560 --> 26:11.020
Exactly.

26:11.200 --> 26:13.380
The helicopter parent looks at the teenager and says,

26:13.380 --> 26:15.460
Look how anxious and fragile they are.

26:15.660 --> 26:17.080
They can't handle the real world.

26:17.240 --> 26:18.460
I must protect them more.

26:18.820 --> 26:20.560
I must intervene on their behalf.

26:20.920 --> 26:24.160
But the teenager is only fragile because of the persistent excess protection.

26:24.780 --> 26:25.840
It's just wild.

26:26.000 --> 26:29.000
Or, you know, the manager micromanages an employee.

26:29.280 --> 26:31.620
The employee never learns to make independent decisions.

26:31.960 --> 26:37.340
And the manager uses that lack of independence as proof that they must continue micromanaging.

26:37.640 --> 26:39.600
It is a perfectly decadent system.

26:39.600 --> 26:44.980
It's acting against its own conditions of production while feeling completely morally justified.

26:45.680 --> 26:48.320
It generates false evidence for its own continuation.

26:49.120 --> 26:53.560
The individuals inside the cocoon genuinely believe they are acting out of care or necessity,

26:54.160 --> 27:01.500
entirely blind to the fact that they are driving the depletion variable, the R, through the roof by preventing the formation of capacity.

27:01.680 --> 27:02.900
It's a tragic loop, really.

27:03.060 --> 27:05.940
Let's pivot this to how we spend our leisure time.

27:06.040 --> 27:06.980
Well, this is a fun one.

27:06.980 --> 27:09.520
The weekend habits of modern life, right?

27:10.140 --> 27:17.440
Binge-watching television, going to massive theme parks, playing 50 hours of video games, taking luxury cruises.

27:18.040 --> 27:20.880
Are these activities inherently decadent?

27:21.040 --> 27:24.480
Well, the paper clarifies this using Nietzsche's own aesthetic values.

27:25.500 --> 27:29.360
The diagnostic criterion for decadence is not the presence of pleasure.

27:29.760 --> 27:31.380
I mean, Nietzsche was not a Puritan.

27:31.620 --> 27:35.180
He profoundly valued music, dance, laughter, and joy.

27:35.340 --> 27:35.540
Right.

27:35.600 --> 27:36.760
He wasn't against having fun.

27:36.880 --> 27:37.560
No, not at all.

27:37.560 --> 27:42.940
The criterion is not the substance of the activity, but its function within the trajectory of your capacities.

27:42.940 --> 27:46.100
It's about what the activity is doing to your internal engine.

27:46.500 --> 27:46.960
Exactly.

27:47.400 --> 27:53.600
If your recreation, whether it's a weekend at a theme park or playing video game, serves as active recovery,

27:53.980 --> 28:00.000
returning you to demanding, difficult work with your capacities restored and your cognitive tension refreshed...

28:00.000 --> 28:02.140
Then it is contributing to regeneration.

28:02.140 --> 28:04.920
It is a functional part of the G variable.

28:04.920 --> 28:05.420
Yes.

28:05.540 --> 28:09.380
But if that exact same activity plays a different functional role...

28:09.380 --> 28:09.500
Right.

28:09.860 --> 28:22.480
If that pleasure substitutes for the tension needed to develop capacity, if you are using it as a permanent anesthetic to escape the friction of life entirely, it shifts from regeneration to depletion.

28:22.480 --> 28:22.960
Wow.

28:22.960 --> 28:33.620
Furthermore, if this escapism requires constantly increasing intensity just to achieve the same baseline of numbing comfort, it is actively consuming your capacity.

28:34.040 --> 28:35.120
It is a decadent arrangement.

28:35.260 --> 28:42.540
So it is the functional difference between having, say, a glass of wine on a Friday night to celebrate the completion of a massive, grueling project...

28:42.540 --> 28:48.200
Versus drinking a bottle of wine every single night, because the reality of your stagnant life is just too painful to face sober.

28:48.500 --> 28:49.580
That's a perfect comparison.

28:50.340 --> 28:57.340
The chemical substance is exactly the same, but its functional role in the trajectory of your life is diametrically opposed.

28:57.580 --> 29:00.800
One is a brief release of tension that allows for future tension.

29:01.440 --> 29:04.660
And the other is the permanent dissolution of tension.

29:04.660 --> 29:11.040
And the text makes a very similar functional assessment of religion, specifically ascetic ideals.

29:11.040 --> 29:19.480
Yes, Meechia viewed the ascetic priest basically as a diagnostician who offered a very specific treatment for human suffering.

29:19.740 --> 29:28.100
Because religion supplies order, it provides a narrative framework that deadens the pain of existence by giving that pain a profound meaning.

29:28.380 --> 29:33.360
Right, like you are suffering because God is testing you, or you are suffering for the sins of the world.

29:33.520 --> 29:35.680
It is an incredibly effective anesthetic.

29:35.840 --> 29:36.300
It is.

29:36.300 --> 29:40.420
But the functional assessment demands, we ask, at what cost?

29:40.800 --> 29:50.200
If a religious arrangement provides relief from suffering, but costs the individual the raw, unmediated friction that might have forced them to develop entirely new capacities.

29:50.480 --> 29:52.520
Then it is contributing to depletion.

29:52.720 --> 29:52.960
Yes.

29:53.500 --> 29:55.860
It keeps the patient alive, but keeps them permanently sick.

29:55.860 --> 29:56.040
Right.

29:56.300 --> 29:59.760
Although the text notes a crucial nuance here, doesn't it?

29:59.760 --> 30:00.460
It does.

30:00.460 --> 30:07.780
Because Nietzsche actually acknowledges that historically, the priestly and ascetic traditions deepened human psychology.

30:07.920 --> 30:08.400
Oh, so?

30:08.760 --> 30:17.700
By forcing raw cruelty inward, they gave humanity a complex inner life that the purely physical warrior cultures just lacked.

30:18.260 --> 30:22.140
So it's a complex ledger of regeneration and depletion.

30:22.140 --> 30:32.400
Okay, so we have spent this entire deep dive using Nietzsche's brutal, subtractive methodology to dismantle our assumptions about history, society, and our own lives.

30:32.580 --> 30:33.240
It's been intense.

30:33.460 --> 30:36.440
But intellectual honesty requires a reflexive check.

30:37.140 --> 30:38.480
We have to turn the mirror around.

30:38.640 --> 30:39.900
Turn his own weapons against him.

30:39.940 --> 30:40.340
Exactly.

30:40.840 --> 30:43.500
Does Nietzsche's grand theory survive if we point it at him?

30:43.560 --> 30:51.100
Well, the paper explicitly conducts this reflexive check, and it points out several profound limitations and contradictions in the Nietzschean reading.

30:51.100 --> 30:52.240
Starting with Rousseau, right.

30:52.540 --> 30:56.000
Yeah, firstly, his portrayal of Rousseau is largely a strategic caricature.

30:56.220 --> 31:06.220
Because in the discourse on inequality, Rousseau wasn't actually arguing that there was a literal historical state of nature that we could find fossil evidence of and return to.

31:06.360 --> 31:13.560
No, he explicitly stated it was a hypothesis, a thought experiment used to isolate the variables of society.

31:13.560 --> 31:23.780
So Nietzsche is really attacking a romanticized tendency that Rousseau inspired in later generations, rather than Rousseau's actual highly nuanced philosophical position.

31:23.900 --> 31:24.300
Precisely.

31:25.000 --> 31:28.400
Furthermore, there is a glaring contradiction in Nietzsche's own vocabulary.

31:29.120 --> 31:31.920
He insists that there is no biological baseline, right?

31:32.000 --> 31:35.260
No original state of perfect health to compare ourselves against.

31:35.460 --> 31:37.840
History is just a series of neutral transformations.

31:37.840 --> 31:44.220
Right. Yet, if you read his texts, he constantly utilizes medical and biological terminology.

31:44.520 --> 31:48.020
He talks about physiological decline, degenerate types, sickness.

31:48.360 --> 31:55.960
Exactly. And you can't use words like degeneration unless you are secretly harboring some metric of what a fully generated or natural baseline looks like.

31:56.040 --> 31:58.100
He smuggles a baseline back into the equation.

31:58.240 --> 32:03.560
He totally does. It sits very uneasily next to his claim of pure, valueless transformations.

32:03.560 --> 32:10.240
But the most revealing application of this reflexive check is when we diagnose the diagnostician himself.

32:10.360 --> 32:11.340
Oh, this is the best part.

32:11.600 --> 32:15.040
I want to apply his exact symptom method back onto him.

32:15.200 --> 32:24.780
Because if the doctrine of natural equality Rousseau's philosophy is just a symptom of resentful, powerless people who cannot handle their lower position in a hierarchy.

32:24.960 --> 32:26.400
Then we have to ask the reverse question.

32:26.400 --> 32:35.480
Right. Isn't Nietzsche's obsession with rank, order, and the preservation of exceptional, powerful individuals just a symptom of his own condition?

32:35.700 --> 32:38.900
The method absolutely demands this exact symmetry.

32:39.360 --> 32:47.600
Yet, remember, Nietzsche was living through the rapid democratization of Europe, the rise of mass culture, and the dissolution of traditional aristocratic hierarchies.

32:47.600 --> 32:55.920
So his entire philosophy of decadence is just the psychological symptom of an elite thinker for whom egalitarian modernity has become intolerable.

32:56.020 --> 33:01.380
Yes. The prospect of a world without rigid hierarchy was psychologically agonizing to him.

33:01.800 --> 33:02.380
So what does he do?

33:02.860 --> 33:08.420
He invents a grand philosophical framework that categorizes equality as a biological sickness.

33:08.900 --> 33:12.920
It is the exact same operation with the direction of the grievance reversed.

33:13.120 --> 33:14.000
Completely reversed.

33:14.000 --> 33:28.120
If the doctrine of equality appeals to the masses as a consolation for their lack of power, the diagnosis of decline appeals to the elite who feel their unique exceptionalism is being suffocated by the rise of the masses.

33:28.400 --> 33:31.540
His philosophy is a symptom of his own specialized resentment.

33:31.840 --> 33:32.240
Exactly.

33:33.100 --> 33:40.380
But, as the paper notes, exposing the psychological ancestry of his valuation doesn't automatically supply a new one.

33:40.380 --> 33:49.420
Right. Because of the genetic fallacy we talked about earlier, pointing out that Nietzsche was anxious about mass culture doesn't negate the mathematical reality of the G-R equation.

33:49.580 --> 33:58.360
No, it doesn't. We are all bound by the forces of history and psychology. No one, not even the great diagnostician, sits outside the causal chain.

33:58.600 --> 34:04.560
Wow. What an immense paradigm-shifting journey this has been. Let's pull all of these threads together for you, the listener.

34:04.560 --> 34:05.800
It's a lot to process.

34:06.140 --> 34:20.840
We started by examining how we measure history, moving from Rousseau's straight line of inevitable corruption and, you know, the nostalgia for that pristine, unscratched smartphone to Nietzsche's dynamic, continuous chain of transformations.

34:21.340 --> 34:30.980
We learned that decadence isn't a state you fall into, but a negative trajectory where a system consumes its own fuel and fails to regenerate its capacities.

34:30.980 --> 34:41.900
And then we traced the psychological alchemy of resentment, watching how raw grievance is magically transformed into universal moral doctrine just to relieve the pain of subordination.

34:42.160 --> 34:51.040
We explored the irreversibility of history, the absolute impossibility of unbaking the cake, and why our only option is not restoration, but revaluation.

34:51.040 --> 35:01.660
And crucially, we applied the mathematics of decadence to our own lives, examining how the protective cocoons we build for ourselves and our children create these self-fulfilling prophecies of fragility.

35:01.840 --> 35:05.200
It completely dismantles the nostalgic, passive way we view the world.

35:05.320 --> 35:11.360
It really does. And it places the burden of capacity generation squarely on how we arrange our present environment.

35:11.360 --> 35:15.240
And so, we want to leave you with this final structural proposition.

35:16.280 --> 35:23.440
We've established that decadence is characterized by a shrinking set of open futures, driven by the systematic removal of tension.

35:23.580 --> 35:27.520
So if you fully accept that you can never go back to a simpler time in your own life.

35:27.600 --> 35:32.760
If there is no innocent past to restore, and no unblemished baseline to recover.

35:32.760 --> 35:44.800
What entirely new, unprecedented configuration of stressors, friction, and sublimation must you actively invent tomorrow to ensure your own capacities continue to regenerate?

35:44.860 --> 35:47.180
Because the historical x-ray machine is broken.

35:47.460 --> 35:50.680
And there is no pristine blueprint to guide you backward.

35:50.980 --> 35:53.580
You have to build the future out of the friction of the present.

35:54.300 --> 35:56.140
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

35:56.340 --> 35:59.560
Remember, the steepest climbs often afford the clearest views.

36:00.000 --> 36:01.280
We will catch you on the next one.

